‘If parody does not prickle it does not work’: Reflections on the Interpretive Challenges of Dark Parody in the Dutch and South African Courts 
Something adds another layer of complexity to interpreting the meaning and impact of a parody.

Someone is tasked with balancing the interest of copyright and trademark rights holders against the parodist’s right to freedom of expression.

Adapting meta-hermeneutics to the nexus of humor and the law, its application to judicial interpretation practices focuses in a certain way. 
Dark humor adds another layer of complexity to interpreting the meaning and impact of a parody.

In parody disputes, judges are tasked with balancing the interest of copyright and trademark rights holders against the parodist’s right to freedom of expression.

Adapting meta-hermeneutics to the nexus of humor and the law, its application to judicial interpretation practices focuses on reconstructing conventional paths judges and courts use to construct meaning.

