**********Source Check**********

The claim 'Conventional wisdom about working-class writing often presupposes a hero employed in a certain way.' is supported by the citation.
<accept> The article explicitly questions and challenges conventional understandings and stereotypes about 'Working Class Writing' and its characters. It mentions how certain historical "characterizations" have "propped up ideas" and "contemporary stereotypes and portrayals" of the working class. Furthermore, it directly addresses and rejects a narrow, conventional definition of the working class (and thus its characters) by quoting Benjamin Myers: "Working class does not mean sink estates, lives lived on hire purchase, teenage pregnancy and so forth. The world has changed and the working class has changed: it cannot be so easily categorisable." This strongly implies that conventional wisdom *did* presuppose a specific type of working-class character, often tied to certain living and employment conditions. The article also explicitly asks "By Working Class, do we mean British people ‘on the dole’? Or do we mean those confined to work in sweatshops in Asia, or those who dig for diamonds for corrupt gangs and governments alike?", which points to common presuppositions about their employment. </accept>

